## 12. Explainability and Qualitative Analysis (RQ3)

[[FIG:results/figures/explainability/fig16_shap_token_attributions|Word-level SHAP attributions for MC7 test comments, explained with respect to the predicted class (red: toward; blue: away). Attributions describe model sensitivity, not author intent. Texts are shown as distributed.|fig:shap]]

**Faithfulness.** Deleting the single word with the highest SHAP value lowers the predicted-class probability by
{{ v.xai.faith.k1.mean_drop_top }} on average, against {{ v.xai.faith.k1.mean_drop_random }} for a random word (100 random
test comments). The difference is {{ v.xai.faith.k1.mean_diff }}, with 95% CI [{{ v.xai.faith.k1.diff_ci95[0] | round(3) }},
{{ v.xai.faith.k1.diff_ci95[1] | round(3) }}]. The top word beats a random one in a share of {{ v.xai.faith.k1.share_top_gt_random }}
of the comments, and the gap persists for $k=2,3$ (Figure \ref{fig:faith}, Table \ref{tab:xai}). By this deletion criterion,
the attributions identify words the model relies on.

[[FIG:results/figures/explainability/fig16b_shap_faithfulness|Deletion test: drop in predicted-class probability after deleting the top-k SHAP words vs. k random words.|fig:faith]]

[[TAB:tab15_explainability|tab:xai]]

**Stability and agreement.** Word rankings at 100 and 250 evaluations agree closely with a 1,000-evaluation reference
(Spearman {{ v.xai.stab.budget_100_vs_1000.spearman_mean }} and {{ v.xai.stab.budget_250_vs_1000.spearman_mean }}). On these
short texts the explainer stops before 1,000 evaluations (525 were used for a 25-token comment), so the 500-vs-1,000
agreement is uninformative and not used as evidence. SHAP agrees only weakly with leave-one-word-out occlusion (Spearman
{{ v.xai.occ.spearman_mean }}, top-3 overlap {{ v.xai.occ.top3_overlap_mean }}). Single-word deletion ignores the
interactions that SHAP's coalitions capture, so the two methods answer different questions. No single attribution method
should be treated as ground truth.

**Qualitative pattern.** In the examples of Figure \ref{fig:shap}, identity and target terms receive the largest
attributions, including in errors. "hindu" dominates a *religion* prediction for a comment annotated *abusive*, and "didi"
(elder sister) drives a *gender* false positive on a harmless comment. This is consistent with reliance on identity terms,
a known source of bias in abuse classifiers \cite{sap2019racial}. It is an observation from selected examples, not a
measured rate.

**Cost.** One explanation takes {{ v.xai.sec }} s on CPU versus {{ v.xai.inf_ms }} ms for a prediction (×{{ v.xai.factor }}).
That is acceptable for on-demand moderator review, but not for explaining every message in a stream.

