## 13. External Evaluation

[[TAB:tab10_external|tab:external]]

Without re-training, the binary MC models transfer poorly to English offensive-language detection (TweetEval/OLID:
MuRIL macro-F1 {{ v.ext.olid_ext__mc2_muril_base.f1 }}, ROC-AUC {{ v.ext.olid_ext__mc2_muril_base.auc }}; TF-IDF SVM
{{ v.ext.olid_ext__mc2_tfidf_svm.f1 }}). This is expected: both the language and the label construct differ. The
Swami-trained sarcasm models reach macro-F1 {{ v.ext.usr_sarcasm__sarcasm_muril_base.f1 }} (ROC-AUC
{{ v.ext.usr_sarcasm__sarcasm_muril_base.auc }}) on the user-supplied, human-labelled sarcasm comments, close to chance.
The two sarcasm corpora therefore do not share a transferable notion of sarcasm, which is another reason the Swami-based
sarcasm signal may not help MC-Hinglish. The user corpus is reported only as a supplementary signal, because its provenance
is undocumented.

